From 17f60acd2749c1d452c461c88d6bfec98b180f1d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Lucas Santos <lsantos7654@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 29 Apr 2026 00:41:30 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 11/24] Background Task mode + cascade cancellation
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Adds `background: true` to the Task tool. When set, Task spawns the
subagent with the same parentID/fork/resume logic as today, but returns
immediately with `task_id` and `status: spawned (background)` instead
of awaiting the prompt. The subagent runs to completion in its own
session; the result is buffered in SessionBackground (a per-parent
in-memory registry keyed by parentID) until the parent consumes it.

The parent picks up results in two steps:
1. On its next turn, the system-array assembly in SessionPrompt scans
   SessionBackground.pendingFor and injects a <system-reminder> listing
   ready task_ids.
2. The model calls a new `read_task_result(task_id)` tool to consume
   each result. Strict pull — running tasks return a status indicator,
   unknown tasks (consumed already or never spawned by this parent)
   return an error.

Cancellation cascade: SessionPrompt.cancel was extended to walk
SessionBackground.runningFor(parentID) and call state.cancel for each
running child, so aborting the parent (existing
POST /:sessionID/abort route) kills running background workers without
any new TUI keybind or HTTP route. Subagents cannot spawn subagents
(task.ts:75,92-100,184-186 deny `task` permission to every Task-spawned
child unless its definition explicitly grants it; no hivemind agent
does), so the cascade is depth-1 — direct calls to state.cancel, no
recursion.

A per-parent concurrency cap of 8 running background tasks is enforced
in SessionBackground.register, which throws CapExceededError; the
Task tool surfaces it as a fail Effect.

New endpoint GET /global/background-tasks returns the active task set
for the upcoming TUI footer indicator. New bus event
session.background.changed fires on every register/complete/consume so
the indicator can update reactively.

Background runners die with the engine process, so the in-memory
registry is consistent with runner lifetime — no persistence layer
needed for v1.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
